WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
- And I'm back.

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.120
Hanging out with David Dobrick.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.530
My next guest prides himself

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.740
on solving problems that don't exist,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.710
by making products nobody's looking for.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.560
Why not?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.410
It's always fun to see
what he comes up with.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.180
Let's get Matt Benedetto in here.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.280
Hey, what's up, man?

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.290
How you doing?

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:15.123
- I'm doing well.

00:00:15.123 --> 00:00:15.956
How are you?

00:00:15.956 --> 00:00:16.789
- I am so good.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:18.920
I love that we've got
you, like, two times over.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.610
This is awesome.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.010
- Yeah, looking great.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:22.843
- Okay.

00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:25.797
All right, so say hi to
David Dobrick as well.

00:00:25.797 --> 00:00:26.630
- Hi Matt, how are you?

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.650
Good to see you.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:28.589
- I'm doing well, how are you David?

00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:29.930
- Pretty good, pretty good.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.640
- All right, well, David
actually loves to buy

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:32.560
random things online.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.570
He just showed me, which I want both.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.500
So we wanted him to see what you've got.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.380
So, Matt, I understand
you've been very busy

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.330
in your studio during the pandemic, right?

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.390
- Yes, this is the unnecessary studio.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.610
Would you like a quick tour?

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.443
- Yes.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.276
- So, here we go.

00:00:45.276 --> 00:00:46.109
So over here,

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.050
we've got a whole slew of 3D printers.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.360
So, I use these to make
some of my inventions.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.970
We have the wall of unnecessary,

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.920
where some of my inventions
end up after I'm done.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.560
Over here, we've got my
little editing and BR station.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.100
I've got a whole sewing station over here.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.210
This is a laser cutter.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.560
So I can use laser to cut,
like, metal and all sorts

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.393
of fun stuff.

00:01:04.393 --> 00:01:05.720
- Wow, that sounds dangerous.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:06.933
But, yes.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.270
- Exactly.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.220
- So, what have you been,
like, you're wearing one of

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.130
your latest things, right?

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:12.963
You said-

00:01:12.963 --> 00:01:13.870
- I am.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.703
So, I-

00:01:14.703 --> 00:01:17.670
This invention came from,
sort of women talking about,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.440
never having enough pocket
space on their pants.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.623
So I thought, instead of two pockets,

00:01:22.623 --> 00:01:26.180
why not have one giant
pocket across your butt?

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.880
And so, David, I know
you like to play tennis,

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.110
so, you know, maybe you could
have your tennis racket there,

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.890
ready to go, but then you
got to get some work done.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.340
You've got your iPad,

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.563
you've got a hairbrush,

00:01:35.665 --> 00:01:37.830
you've got a sandwich for lunch.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.950
Everything you need, everything.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:40.980
- I was just going to say,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.900
I have had the toughest
time fitting my racket

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.100
into my pockets.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.890
(laughs)

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.120
- I just love that, if you
knew the size of my ass,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.396
a pocket on my ass-

00:01:51.396 --> 00:01:52.462
(laughs)

00:01:52.462 --> 00:01:54.350
- Well, it sort of gives
whole new meaning to,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.342
junk in the trunk.

00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:57.226
- Yeah, it's a lot of more.

00:01:57.226 --> 00:01:58.290
Its a (indistinct) thing.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.600
That's amazing.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.620
All right, well, Matt sent David and me,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.660
another one of his practical inventions.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.420
It is a necklace and
I'm very curious, okay.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.253
It's very-

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:09.086
It's a statement.

00:02:09.086 --> 00:02:09.960
- We want you to have one.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.810
It connects in the back.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:12.790
- Its warm.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.190
- And so, you know, when you're
buying some chicken nuggets,

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.558
you know, you want to get the 20 piece,

00:02:16.558 --> 00:02:18.800
but sometimes you can't eat them all.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.760
So, this is called the Nuggie Necklace.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.530
So, each container magnetically comes off,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.620
and you have a chicken nugget.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.880
So, you can have maybe only half of it,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.670
put the other half back, snap it back in.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.400
- How long has this been in here?

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.660
- Yeah, I know.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.300
- I shipped them like
four, three weeks ago.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.206
They should be fine.

00:02:37.206 --> 00:02:38.120
- [Kelly] Oh, they're fine.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.326
- Thank God Nuggets don't
go bad for like, five years.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.159
(laughs)

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.040
- I'm not even kidding you.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.160
My kids would love this.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.530
This is, this is amazing.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.150
So, how is your Nuggie necklace?

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.232
Do you like it, David?

00:02:48.232 --> 00:02:49.065
(laughs)

00:02:49.065 --> 00:02:50.700
- Yeah, I'm obsessed with it.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.300
I would like the Nuggie
belt too, just in case.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:54.937
So I can have more Nuggets.

00:02:54.937 --> 00:02:56.660
- Across the chest, around the waist,

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.790
just like 49 Nuggets, nonstop.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.180
- So wait, Matt.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.880
Wait, what's the last invention
you have for us today?

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.050
- So the very last one.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.880
So, I just won the Webby for
the Social Weird Category.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.160
And it was my very first award that I won.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.097
So I thought,

00:03:11.097 --> 00:03:14.133
I want everyone to know,
that I won an award.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.040
I built a harness to show
off my Webby award, nonstop.

00:03:20.375 --> 00:03:23.540
I heard that I should be
making a separate one,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.869
that's the Emmy harness,
because I believe we have a-

00:03:26.869 --> 00:03:27.703
- Emmy!

00:03:27.703 --> 00:03:28.536
I did.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.293
- Congratulations.

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:31.680
- I can't believe it, I'm still shocked.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.330
I think it might've been
an accident, but it's fine.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:35.910
I love that it looks like you've got

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.900
a baby Bjorn on, from a (indistinct).

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.080
It looks- from my perspective.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:40.930
- So there we go.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.721
So, the Webby harness.

00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:45.260
On the strap, it just
says, Look at my award.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.100
'Cause I just want everyone to know,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:47.933
that I, now,

00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:50.490
am a award winning weirdo.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.503
- Oh my gosh, wait.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.580
This is a real thing.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.310
So, we're about to tape The Voice,

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.760
and I totally want to have that,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.980
so I can put my Voice Trophies on them,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.850
and just walk around Blake Shelton,

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:01.944
and just be like,

00:04:01.944 --> 00:04:03.226
" oh, these things?"

00:04:03.226 --> 00:04:04.059
- Put some lights on.

00:04:04.059 --> 00:04:04.892
- Fun.

00:04:04.892 --> 00:04:06.340
Yes, that's what I'm
going to do on The Voice.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.900
That would be amazing.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.123
Would you wear that harness, David?

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.570
- Again, I already have-

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:13.820
I already have too much.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.248
Too much eyes on me.

00:04:15.248 --> 00:04:16.110
- Too much eyes on me.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:16.943
He likes the Nuggie neglace.

00:04:16.943 --> 00:04:18.567
- I'd like to stay with this one.

00:04:19.657 --> 00:04:22.360
- All right, so you,
you made an invention,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:23.280
that people can buy.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.720
What is it, Matt?

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.960
- So, yeah, so I just launched.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:30.100
So, after 180 inventions over
the past year and a half,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.180
I launched the Jigsaw Puzzle coffee table.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.620
So, the top of the coffee table,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.000
can't be used until you
solve the jigsaw puzzle.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.529
So, it's sort of like, Ikea
directions on steroids.

00:04:40.529 --> 00:04:42.733
You have to put the entire puzzle

00:04:42.733 --> 00:04:44.720
of the coffee table together,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.350
before you can actually use
this functional coffee too.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.110
- Okay, well, nothing on Ikea,

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.010
but I'm just telling you,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.598
the people that should
make directions to build

00:04:51.598 --> 00:04:55.900
anything in life, or whoever
does the directions for Legos,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.250
those people have it going on.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.550
It's so easy.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.050
Thank you so much, Matt and David.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.160
Ya'll check out Matt's book

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.530
101 Unnecessary Inventions.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.970
The new updated version
will be out this fall.

